Plenum Bop
Ingredients: NU JAZZ / ELECTRO JAZZ + EUPHORIC, ENERGETIC & JOLLY + Violin  
Creative Catalyst: It was invented two kilometers underground by salt miners drumming frantic, syncopated polyrhythms across corrugated steel ventilation pipes during cage elevator delays.

Plenum Bop is a radiant, breathless collision of broken-beat electro jazz and virtuosic fiddle-driven swing, anchored by the bright acoustic resonance of subterranean tin-pipe percussion. High-flying, rosin-dusted violin runs leap through warm chords played on a saturated Fender Rhodes, while skipping 128-BPM drum programming collides with the metallic ping and hollow bloom of ventilation ducting. The result is unpretentiously ecstatic: music designed to push stale air out of deep chambers with sheer kinetic joy.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 124–130 BPM; an upbeat, syncopated broken-beat groove borrowing from London west-end broken beat and traditional Polish oberek swing, driven forward by hollow, tuned metallic thuds.
- Harmonic Palette: Sun-drenched Lydian and Mixolydian modes, rich maj9/13 jazz extensions, and modal gypsy-jazz scale runs that resolve into buoyant, whistle-along turnarounds.
- Production Signatures: 
  1. Acoustic Duct Convolution: Custom reverb impulses recorded inside three-foot-wide zinc ventilation tubes, creating a signature resonant slapback.
  2. Bowed Sidechaining: Micro-mic’d violin tracks violently sidechained to the offbeat pipe-strikes, making the strings pulse like a brass section.
  3. Galvanized Tubing Bass: Sub-bass tones derived from pitching down hollow pipe strikes, layered beneath a walking upright acoustic bass.
- Dynamics: Explosive drop-ins from solo acoustic violin into wall-of-sound rhythmic bounce, featuring sudden two-bar "draft breaks" where all drums drop out for a burst of four-part vocal scats before slamming back into full euphoria.

Scene & Origin Lore
Plenum Bop coalesced in the deep potash and salt shafts of Upper Silesia around 2011, when trapped night-shift drilling crews passed six-hour lift-motor outages by rhythmically beating ductwork with ball-peen hammers, improvising call-and-response melodies to keep carbon monoxide monitors out of mind. When fiddler and surface engineer Dariusz "Darek" Kłos brought a condenser mic and an MPC down the hoist shaft, those pipe patterns were transferred to late-night dance clubs in Katowice and Berlin. The scene quickly forged its own look: tailored vintage jazz suits cut from fire-retardant blue drill-cotton, pocket squares stained with industrial chalk, and brass pipe-fittings worn as lapel pins. Club nights are notoriously athletic; dancers chalk the soles of their dress shoes to scuff intricate patterns onto oiled warehouse floorboards while the violinist plays from atop suspended air conduits.

Typical Instruments: Solo acoustic violin (often fitted with piezo bridge pickups), Fender Rhodes Mark II, pitched galvanized steel pipes, upright bass, Roland TR-909 rimshots, Oberheim OB-Xa synths, and shouted communal choruses.

For Fans Of: Bugz in the Attic, Jean-Luc Ponty, Caravan Palace, Jazzanova, St Germain.

